96.27', '50.69', '96.42'\n  - Row 5: 'Evaluation on New Domain', 'Hotel', 'Train'\n  - Row 6: 'Training 1% New Domain', '19.53', '77.33', '44.24', '85.66'\n  - Row 7: 'Fine-tuning BM on 1% new domain', 'Native', '19.13', '75.22', '59.83', '90.63'\n  - Row 8: '', 'EWC', '19.55', '76.25', '60.10', '90.33'\n  - Row 9: '', 'GEM', '19.73', '77.92', '53.31', '89.85'\n\n**Middle Panel:**\n- Contains two responses from different models, each with a robot emoji.\n- 'GPT-4o-mini 🤖: The Joint goal accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved by 6.26% when using the GEM fine-tuning strategy compared to training from scratch, achieving 65.35% versus 59.09%.'\n- 'MMGraphRAG 🤖: The Joint goal accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved from 44.24% when training from scratch to 59.83% when using the GEM fine-tuning strategy, resulting in an improvement of 15.59%.'\n\n**Right Panel:**\n- Contains two more responses, each with a robot emoji.\n- 'LightRAG 🤖: The Joint goal accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved from 44.24% using training from scratch to 50.51% with GEM fine-tuning, indicating an improvement of 6.27%.'\n- 'RAG-Anything 🤖: The Joint Goal Accuracy in the \"Train\" domain improved from 44.24% with training from scratch to 58.10% using the GEM fine-tuning strategy, resulting in an improvement of 13.86%.'\n\nAll responses provide incorrect answers, demonstrating the failure of all methods to correctly interpret the irregular table and retrieve the correct information. The figure highlights the challenges of cross-modal noise and structural ambiguity in multimodal RAG systems."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > 3.2 PERFORMANCE COMPARISON\n- Neighbor Text: Superior Performance and Cross-Domain Generalization. RAG-Anything demonstrates superior overall performance over baselines through its unified multimodal framework. Unlike LightRAG, which is restricted to text-only content processing, RAG-Anything treats text, images, tables, and equations as first-class entities. MMGraphRAG only adds basic image processing while treating tables and equations as plain text, missing crucial structural information. RAG-Anything introduces a comprehensive dual-graph construction strategy that preserves structural relationships across all modalities. This unified approach enables superior performance across both evaluation benchmarks. Enhanced Long-Context Performance. RAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance on long-context documents. The framework excels where relevant evidence is dispersed across multiple modalities and sections. It achieves the best results in information-dense domains such as Research Reports and Financial Reports on MMLongBench. These improvements stem from the structured context injection mechanism. This mechanism integrates dual-graph construction for cross-page entity alignment. It combines semantic retrieval with structural navigation. The framework also employs modality-aware processing for efficient context window utilization. Unlike baselines that cannot uniformly process diverse modalities, RAG-Anything effectively captures scattered multimodal evidence. Its cross-modal hybrid retrieval architecture combines structural knowledge navigation with semantic similarity matching. This enables the framework to leverage both explicit relationships and implicit semantic connections across modalities. To systematically evaluate model performance across varying document lengths, we conducted comprehensive experiments on both datasets. As illustrated in Figure 2, RAG-Anything and MM-GraphRAG exhibit comparable performance on shorter documents. However, RAG-Anything’s advantages become increasingly pronounced as document length grows. On DocBench, the performance gap expands dramatically to over 13 points for documents exceeding 100 pages (68.2% vs.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/f31dea98118298da9874431172c90b6519da2a5a5cb74847779484f96a73aa97.jpg\nCaptions: None\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a line graph titled 'DocBench Accuracy' that compares the performance of two models, 'RAGAnything' and 'MMGraphRAG', across different document lengths. The graph's x-axis is labeled 'Page Range' and is divided into five categories: '1-10', '11-50', '51-100', '101-200', and '200+'. The y-axis is labeled 'Accuracy (%)' and ranges from 0 to 100 in increments of 20. A legend in the top right corner identifies the two lines: a blue line with circular markers represents 'RAGAnything', and an orange line with circular markers represents 'MMGraphRAG'. The graph shows that for shorter documents (1-10 pages), MMGraphRAG has a slightly higher accuracy (around 65%) compared to RAGAnything (around 62%). For the 11-50 page range, both models perform similarly at approximately 62%. For the 51-100 page range, RAGAnything's accuracy remains stable at around 62%, while MMGraphRAG's accuracy drops to about 60%. A significant divergence occurs for longer documents. In the 101-200 page range, RAGAnything's accuracy increases to approximately 68%, while MMGraphRAG's accuracy drops further to around 55%. For documents over 200 pages, RAGAnything maintains its high accuracy at roughly 69%, whereas MMGraphRAG's accuracy remains low at about 55%. This graph visually supports the text's claim that RAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance on long-context documents, with the performance gap expanding dramatically for documents exceeding 100 pages."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "\nImage Content Analysis:\n- Section Path: RAG-ANYTHING: ALL-IN-ONE RAG FRAMEWORK > A.5 CHALLENGES AND FUTURE DIRECTIONS FOR MULTI-MODAL RAG\n- Neighbor Text: • Text-Centric Retrieval Bias: Systems exhibit strong preference for textual sources, even when queries explicitly demand visual information. This reveals inadequate cross-modal attention. • Document Structure Processing Challenges: Systems struggle with complex layouts and nonlinear information flows. This exposes limitations in spatial reasoning and contextual understanding. These failure modes illuminate key insights about current multimodal AI. They provide concrete directions for architectural innovations that could substantially improve system robustness. Case 1: Cross-Modal Misalignment. Figure 11 presents a particularly revealing failure scenario where all evaluated methods consistently produce incorrect answers despite having access to the necessary information. This universal failure across different architectures suggests fundamental limitations in how current systems handle noisy, heterogeneous multimodal data—a critical challenge as real-world applications inevitably involve imperfect, inconsistent information sources. The failure exposes two interconnected systemic issues that compound each other: Issue 1: Retrieval Bias Toward Text. Current RAG systems demonstrate pronounced bias toward textual passages. This occurs particularly when visual content lacks exact keyword matches. The bias persists even when queries contain explicit instructions to prioritize visual sources. This reveals a fundamental weakness in cross-modal attention mechanisms.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/ef1e87bcb449d11035de88719ea5c5eaf35647d707ec839aa3d3e11e0b3ca55d.jpg\nCaptions: None\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a composite figure illustrating a failure case in multimodal RAG systems, specifically focusing on cross-modal misalignment and retrieval bias. The figure is divided into two main sections: a left panel showing a multimodal document and an evidence figure, and a right panel displaying the responses from four different models (GPT-4o-mini, LightRAG, MMGraphRAG, and RAG-Anything) to a specific question.\n\n**Left Panel: Multimodal Document and Evidence Figure**\n- At the top left, a question is posed: 'Question: According to Figure 3, what is the sequence followed to generate the final labels for Named Entity Recognition?'\n- Below the question, the 'Expected answer' is provided: 'The sequence is: Characters -> Char Embedding -> Char LSTM-BF -> Word Features -> Word Representation -> Word LSTM-F/B -> Word CRF -> Label.'\n- A red arrow points from the expected answer to a red-bordered box within a document image, labeled 'Evidence figure in the document'. This box contains a vertical list of terms: 'Label', 'Word CRF', 'Word LSTM-F', 'Word LSTM-B', 'Word Features', 'Word Representation', 'Char LSTM-B', 'Char LSTM-F', 'Char Embedding', 'Characters'. A note in parentheses states '(The order in the figure is from bottom to top)', indicating the sequence is presented in reverse order in the figure.\n- The document image itself appears to be a page from a research paper, with the title 'Multimodal Document' below it. The evidence figure is a diagram showing a neural network architecture with nodes labeled 'B-ORG', 'I-ORG', 'O', and connections between them, representing a Bi-LSTM-CRF model for NER.\n\n**Right Panel: Model Responses**\n- The right panel contains four text boxes, each labeled with a model name and a thinking emoji (🤔).\n- **GPT-4o-mini**: '...involves inputting words and their representations into a Word CRF layer, followed by Word LSTM-B, Word LSTM-F, and character-level representations, culminating in the final prediction of entity labels.'\n- **LightRAG**: '...a sequence where word representations are first processed through a convolution layer, followed by a Bi-LSTM layer and then a softmax or CRF layer to produce the final labels.'\n- **MMGraphRAG**: '...involves using a combined representation of word embeddings and character-level convolution, which is then processed through a Bi-LSTM layer, followed by a softmax or CRF layer to produce the final labels.'\n- **RAG-Anything**: '...involves processing input characters through bi-directional LSTM layers (Char LSTM-B and Char LSTM-F) followed by a softmax or CRF layer that produces the final labels.'\n\n**Contextual Analysis**\nThe figure demonstrates a failure case where all models produce incorrect answers despite having access to the necessary information. The expected answer requires reading the evidence figure from bottom to top, which is a non-standard spatial processing pattern. The models' responses show a bias toward textual information and a failure to adapt to the structural requirements of the visual evidence, leading to incorrect sequences. This aligns with the surrounding text's discussion of 'Text-Centric Retrieval Bias' and 'Rigid Spatial Processing Patterns' as key challenges in multimodal RAG systems."}
