{"entity": "RAG-Anything", "type": "method", "description": "RAG-Anything is a unified, all-in-one retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) framework designed to reimagine multimodal knowledge representation and retrieval. It treats text, images, tables, and equations as first-class entities, reconceptualizing multimodal content as interconnected knowledge entities. The framework extends GraphRAG to all modalities and unifies heterogeneous multimodal knowledge by decomposing content into atomic content units.\n\nAt its core, RAG-Anything employs a dual-graph construction strategy, building both cross-modal and text-based knowledge graphs that are merged via entity alignment. This produces modality-aware graphs that preserve essential relationships within multimodal documents, enabling precise reasoning over complex layouts. Complementing this is a cross-modal hybrid retrieval mechanism, which together with the dual-graph approach forms the framework's central architecture for cross-modal retrieval and reasoning. The framework also includes a dense representation generation step in its indexing pipeline and constructs a cross-modal knowledge graph (as described by Equation 4). Additional features include context-aware multimodal prompting.\n\nRAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance on long documents and complex multimodal layouts, particularly where relevant evidence is dispersed across multiple modalities and sections. It has been evaluated using the DocBench and MMLongBench benchmarks, including an ablation study on DocBench. On MMLongBench, it achieves the best results in information-dense domains such as Research Reports and Financial Reports, with consistent improvements across all document length categories: accuracy gains of 3.4 points for 11–50 pages, 9.3 points for 51–100 pages, and 7.9 points for 101–200 pages. It outperforms MM-GraphRAG on long-document reasoning tasks.\n\nThe framework excels at complex visual reasoning and tabular navigation by constructing structured graph representations of visual elements (such as bar plots) and tabular data, enabling precise alignment between visual and textual information. However, in documented failure cases, RAG-Anything produced an incorrect answer regarding the NER label generation sequence and failed to correctly interpret an irregular table in a cross-modal noise scenario. The framework's documentation also addresses challenges and future directions for multimodal RAG."}
